The upload step on the run that started at 10:24 AM succeeded, so the bucket should now have a file named like `supabase_backup_20261022_142451.sql.gz`. That file is a gzipped SQL dump of the `postgres` database. Restore it into a throwaway Postgres and query that. Leave the live Supabase database alone.

Download it with the same R2 credentials the workflow uses:

```bash
export AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID="$R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID"
export AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY="$R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY"
export AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=auto
export AWS_REQUEST_CHECKSUM_CALCULATION=WHEN_REQUIRED
export AWS_RESPONSE_CHECKSUM_VALIDATION=WHEN_REQUIRED

aws s3 ls "s3://$R2_BUCKET_NAME/" \
  --endpoint-url "$R2_ENDPOINT_URL" \
  --region auto

aws s3 cp "s3://$R2_BUCKET_NAME/supabase_backup_20261022_142521.sql.gz" ./backup.sql.gz \
  --endpoint-url "$R2_ENDPOINT_URL" \
  --region auto
```

Use the name from `aws s3 ls` if the timestamp differs. Then check the file and load it into a disposable database on port 5433:

```bash
gunzip -t backup.sql.gz

docker run --name hustix-backup-test \
  -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=backup-test \
  -p 5433:5432 -d postgres:17

gunzip -c backup.sql.gz | psql "postgresql://postgres:backup-test@127.0.0.1:5433/postgres"
```

Some lines will error because the dump refers to Supabase roles such as `supabase_admin` and `authenticator`. Those errors are expected in a plain Postgres container. The test that matters is whether your tables and rows are there:

```bash
psql "postgresql://postgres:backup-test@127.0.0.1:5433/postgres" -c \
  "SELECT count(*) FROM information_schema.tables WHERE table_schema = 'public';"
```

Pick one table you know has rows, such as `public.agreement_versions`, and `SELECT count(*)` from it. Compare that count with the live database. When the counts match, remove the throwaway container:

```bash
docker rm -f hustix-backup-test
```

A real restore uses the same `gunzip | psql` command against a new empty Supabase project. Run that only when you mean to replace that project.